00:00:46.863 --> 00:00:48.783
Introduction and Why It Matters.

12
00:00:50.033 --> 00:00:52.508
A story: we added pods and it got slower.

13
00:00:52.788 --> 00:00:55.689
The grocery platform had grown 10 times in a year.

14
00:00:55.969 --> 00:01:05.911
For the festival sale, the cart team did what had always worked: they raised the Horizontal Pod Autoscaler's maximum from 20 to 80 pods for cart-service.

15
00:01:06.191 --> 00:01:12.697
At 8:00 pm the HPA did its job and scaled from 20 to 64 pods.

16
00:01:12.977 --> 00:01:21.041
Cart throughput dropped from ~17,000 to ~13,000 requests per second, and p99 latency tripled.

17
00:01:21.321 --> 00:01:23.988
Three hidden costs grew faster than the pod count:

18
00:01:24.268 --> 00:01:25.207
Contention.

19
00:01:25.487 --> 00:01:31.695
Every add to cart took a Redis lock on a global cart:config key to read the per-customer item limit.

20
00:01:31.975 --> 00:01:34.300
More pods meant more waiters on the same key.

21
00:01:35.650 --> 00:01:36.674
Coherency.

22
00:01:36.954 --> 00:01:44.463
Each pod held an in-memory promotion cache and broadcast an invalidation to every other pod whenever a promotion changed.

23
00:01:44.743 --> 00:01:48.903
With N pods that's N times (N minus 1) messages per change.

24
00:01:49.183 --> 00:01:55.455
At 64 pods, promotion churn during the sale alone generated millions of messages per minute.

25
00:01:55.735 --> 00:01:56.866
Connection limits.

26
00:01:57.146 --> 00:02:09.647
64 pods times a PostgreSQL pool of 20 = 1,280 connections against a maxconnections of 500 (behind PgBouncer, which was now queueing).

27
00:02:09.927 --> 00:02:13.874
Cart snapshots to PostgreSQL waited seconds for a connection.

28
00:02:15.224 --> 00:02:17.869
Scaling back to 40 pods improved throughput.

29
00:02:18.149 --> 00:02:31.739
Afterwards, a 30-minute load test at 1, 2, 4, 8, 16 and 24 pods, fitted with the Universal Scalability Law, would have predicted this: peak capacity at about 43 pods, then decline.

30
00:02:33.089 --> 00:02:33.771
Quote:

31
00:02:34.051 --> 00:02:35.715
The thesis of this chapter.

32
00:02:35.995 --> 00:02:40.006
Horizontal scaling is a property you design in, not a knob you turn.

33
00:02:40.286 --> 00:02:49.758
A system scales only as far as its shared parts allow: the locks everyone waits on, the coordination everyone does, and the connections everyone holds.

34
00:02:50.038 --> 00:02:54.667
Find those, remove or partition them, and measure before you buy capacity.

35
