80
00:05:02.825 --> 00:05:04.702
Core Concepts, Beginner to Deep.

81
00:05:05.952 --> 00:05:09.301
Intuition: billing counters and the manager's approval.

82
00:05:09.581 --> 00:05:12.589
A supermarket adds billing counters for the festival.

83
00:05:12.869 --> 00:05:15.301
Throughput rises, until two things happen:

84
00:05:15.581 --> 00:05:19.955
Every bill over 5,000 rupeesneeds the floor manager's signature.

85
00:05:20.235 --> 00:05:24.907
One manager, many counters: queues form at the manager, not at the counters.

86
00:05:25.187 --> 00:05:26.787
That's contention (σ).

87
00:05:27.067 --> 00:05:30.928
Every counter must tell every other counter whenever a price changes.

88
00:05:31.208 --> 00:05:33.576
With 5 counters that's 20 messages.

89
00:05:33.856 --> 00:05:41.067
With 40 counters it's 1,560, and cashiers spend their time on messages instead of customers.

90
00:05:41.347 --> 00:05:43.096
That's coherency (κ).

91
00:05:44.446 --> 00:05:47.369
Adding counters helps while counters are the bottleneck.

92
00:05:47.649 --> 00:05:51.809
Past a point, it makes the queue at the manager longer and the chatter louder.

93
00:05:52.089 --> 00:05:53.689
The fix isn't more counters.

94
00:05:53.969 --> 00:06:01.073
It's more managers (partition the approval) and fewer broadcasts (publish price changes to a board everyone reads).

