Route by partition key; read models.

259
00:13:23.685 --> 00:13:28.229
Connection pools to one DB. σ or κ: σ + κ.

260
00:13:28.509 --> 00:13:29.383
Example:

261
00:13:29.663 --> 00:13:32.543
Pods times pool the DB's useful concurrency.

262
00:13:32.823 --> 00:13:33.549
Fix:

263
00:13:33.829 --> 00:13:36.986
Pool budgets, PgBouncer (Ch 05).

264
00:13:38.336 --> 00:13:39.083
Warning:

265
00:13:39.363 --> 00:13:41.773
Fitting the USL needs real measurements.

266
00:13:42.053 --> 00:13:53.851
Measure throughput at several pod counts (1, 2, 4, 8, 16,...) with the same workload mix, with each run saturated (enough load to keep the pods busy) and long enough to be stable.

267
00:13:54.131 --> 00:13:56.200
Fit σ and κ, then predict.

268
00:13:56.480 --> 00:14:03.691
Treat the prediction as a warning light, not a guarantee: the model assumes the bottlenecks don't change shape between measurements.

269
00:14:05.041 --> 00:14:06.385
Stateless services.

270
00:14:06.665 --> 00:14:13.875
A service is stateless when any instance can serve any request, and losing an instance loses nothing but in-flight work.

271
00:14:14.155 --> 00:14:17.825
State still exists; it just lives in a store designed to hold it.

272
00:14:19.175 --> 00:14:20.519
Here is a quick table:

273
00:14:20.799 --> 00:14:24.127
Hidden state, and Why it breaks scaling, and Where it should live.

274
00:14:24.407 --> 00:14:26.369
In-memory HTTP sessions.

275
00:14:26.649 --> 00:14:27.972
Why it breaks scaling:

276
00:14:28.252 --> 00:14:31.836
Sticky sessions required; a pod loss logs users out.

277
00:14:32.116 --> 00:14:33.204
Where it should live:

278
00:14:33.484 --> 00:14:35.980
Redis (Spring Session) or signed tokens.

279
00:14:36.260 --> 00:14:37.561
Carts in pod memory.

280
00:14:37.841 --> 00:14:39.164
Why it breaks scaling:

281
00:14:39.444 --> 00:14:41.236
Lost on scale-in or deploy.

282
00:14:41.516 --> 00:14:42.604
Where it should live:

283
00:14:42.884 --> 00:14:44.740
Redis (+ DB snapshot).

284
00:14:45.020 --> 00:14:46.940
Uploaded files on local disk.

285
00:14:47.220 --> 00:14:48.543
Why it breaks scaling:

286
00:14:48.823 --> 00:14:50.316
Only one pod has them.

287
00:14:50.596 --> 00:14:51.684
Where it should live:

288
00:14:51.964 --> 00:14:54.396
Object storage (S3-compatible).

289
00:14:54.676 --> 00:14:57.151
In-process caches without invalidation.

290
00:14:57.431 --> 00:14:58.753
Why it breaks scaling:

291
00:14:59.033 --> 00:15:01.529
Pods disagree (Ch 07).

292
00:15:01.809 --> 00:15:02.897
Where it should live:

293
00:15:03.177 --> 00:15:06.356
Short TTL + broadcast, or an external cache.

294
00:15:06.636 --> 00:15:09.004
Scheduled jobs running on every pod.

295
00:15:09.284 --> 00:15:10.607
Why it breaks scaling:

296
00:15:10.887 --> 00:15:13.276
Duplicate reminders, double processing.

297
00:15:13.556 --> 00:15:14.644
Where it should live:

298
00:15:14.924 --> 00:15:18.380
Leader election or a job lock (§2.7).

299
00:15:18.660 --> 00:15:19.983
Per-pod rate limits.

300
00:15:20.263 --> 00:15:21.585
Why it breaks scaling:

301
00:15:21.865 --> 00:15:23.849
Limit multiplies with pod count.

302
00:15:24.129 --> 00:15:25.217
Where it should live:

303
00:15:25.497 --> 00:15:27.695
Central limiter (Ch 08).

304
00:15:29.045 --> 00:15:30.858
Local counters and sequences.

305
00:15:31.138 --> 00:15:32.461
Why it breaks scaling:

306
00:15:32.741 --> 00:15:33.999
Duplicate ids.

307
00:15:34.279 --> 00:15:35.367
Where it should live:

308
00:15:35.647 --> 00:15:39.637
DB sequences, UUIDv7 or Snowflake (Ch 20).

309
00:15:39.917 --> 00:15:42.157
WebSocket or SSE connections.

310
00:15:42.437 --> 00:15:43.759
Why it breaks scaling:

311
00:15:44.039 --> 00:15:45.469
Inherent per connection.

312
00:15:45.749 --> 00:15:46.837
Where it should live:

313
00:15:47.117 --> 00:15:50.615
Resumable streams, no stickiness (Ch 03).

314
00:15:51.965 --> 00:16:10.909
The 12-factor rules that matter most for scaling: processes are stateless and share nothing (factor VI), config comes from the environment (III), backing services are attached resources (IV), and processes are disposable, meaning fast startup and graceful shutdown (IX).

315
00:16:12.259 --> 00:16:13.817
Session externalization.

316
00:16:14.097 --> 00:16:15.441
Here is a quick table:

317
00:16:15.721 --> 00:16:18.601
Option, and How, and Pros, and Cons, and Use.

318
00:16:18.881 --> 00:16:19.990
Sticky sessions.

319
00:16:20.270 --> 00:16:20.931
How:

320
00:16:21.211 --> 00:16:23.046
LB pins a user to a pod.

321
00:16:23.326 --> 00:16:24.179
Pros:

322
00:16:24.459 --> 00:16:25.675
No code change.

323
00:16:25.955 --> 00:16:26.766
Cons:

324
00:16:27.046 --> 00:16:31.121
Uneven load; lost on pod loss or deploy (Ch 08).

325
00:16:31.401 --> 00:16:32.105
Use:

326
00:16:32.385 --> 00:16:33.217
Avoid.

327
00:16:33.497 --> 00:16:35.374
Replicated in-memory sessions.

328
00:16:35.654 --> 00:16:36.315
How:

329
00:16:36.595 --> 00:16:38.729
App servers copy sessions to peers.

330
00:16:39.009 --> 00:16:39.862
Pros:

331
00:16:40.142 --> 00:16:41.465
No external store.

332
00:16:41.745 --> 00:16:45.307
Cons: κ grows with cluster size; memory times N.

333
00:16:45.587 --> 00:16:46.291
Use:

334
00:16:46.571 --> 00:16:47.659
Avoid at scale.

335
00:16:47.939 --> 00:16:49.390
External session store.

336
00:16:49.670 --> 00:16:50.331
How:

337
00:16:50.611 --> 00:16:53.321
Spring Session with Redis (or JDBC).

338
00:16:53.601 --> 00:16:54.454
Pros:

339
00:16:54.734 --> 00:16:57.763
Any pod serves any request; survives restarts.

340
00:16:58.043 --> 00:16:58.854
Cons:

341
00:16:59.134 --> 00:17:02.355
A Redis round trip per request; session blob size.

342
00:17:02.635 --> 00:17:03.339
Use:

343
00:17:03.619 --> 00:17:05.262
Web apps with server sessions.

344
00:17:05.542 --> 00:17:07.931
Client-side tokens (JWT).

345
00:17:08.211 --> 00:17:08.873
How:

346
00:17:09.153 --> 00:17:11.265
Signed claims travel with each request.

347
00:17:11.545 --> 00:17:12.398
Pros:

348
00:17:12.678 --> 00:17:14.406
Zero server session lookups.

349
00:17:14.686 --> 00:17:15.497
Cons:

350
00:17:15.777 --> 00:17:19.425
Revocation is harder; size limits; claims must be small.

351
00:17:19.705 --> 00:17:20.409
Use:

352
00:17:20.689 --> 00:17:24.379
APIs and mobile apps (Ch 08, 23).

353
00:17:25.729 --> 00:17:35.073
For the grocery platform: identity is a JWT validated at the gateway (Ch 08), so there's no server-side login session.

354
00:17:35.353 --> 00:17:37.145
The cart is not session data.

355
00:17:37.425 --> 00:17:46.364
It's first-class state keyed by customer (or by a device-issued guest cart id) in Redis, with a periodic snapshot to PostgreSQL.

356
00:17:46.644 --> 00:17:54.047
That distinction matters: a session blob is rewritten whole on every change and can't be updated atomically from two tabs.

357
00:17:54.327 --> 00:17:59.767
Guest cart, then customer cart merge is the classic correctness problem of externalized state:

358
00:18:01.117 --> 00:18:04.743
A guest adds items on the web, then logs in with OTP.

359
00:18:05.023 --> 00:18:07.946
The customer already has a cart from the app last week.

360
00:18:08.226 --> 00:18:15.543
Both carts must merge exactly once, atomically, even if two pods receive login and add item at the same moment.

361
00:18:15.823 --> 00:18:21.263
L3 implements it as a single Redis Lua script with a merge marker for idempotency.

362
00:18:22.613 --> 00:18:25.984
Scheduled jobs and singletons in a horizontal world.

363
00:18:26.264 --> 00:18:29.187
With 40 pods, @Scheduled runs 40 times.

364
00:18:30.537 --> 00:18:31.881
Here is a quick table:

365
00:18:32.161 --> 00:18:33.718
Approach, and How, and When.

366
00:18:33.998 --> 00:18:38.009
Job lock (ShedLock, Redis SET NX PX, DB row lock).

367
00:18:38.289 --> 00:18:38.950
How:

368
00:18:39.230 --> 00:18:42.558
Each run acquires a named lock with a lease; others skip.

369
00:18:42.838 --> 00:18:43.457
When:

370
00:18:43.737 --> 00:18:46.723
Periodic jobs that must run once per interval.

371
00:18:47.003 --> 00:18:50.502
Leader election (Kubernetes Lease, ZooKeeper or etcd).

372
00:18:50.782 --> 00:18:51.443
How:

373
00:18:51.723 --> 00:18:54.155
One pod is leader and runs singletons.

374
00:18:54.435 --> 00:18:55.054
When:

375
00:18:55.334 --> 00:18:57.318
Long-running singleton processes.

376
00:18:57.598 --> 00:19:01.161
Partitioned work (FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED, Kafka partitions).

377
00:19:01.441 --> 00:19:02.102
How:

378
00:19:02.382 --> 00:19:04.366
Every pod takes a disjoint slice.

379
00:19:04.646 --> 00:19:05.265
When:

380
00:19:05.545 --> 00:19:07.913
Large batches that should use all pods.

381
00:19:08.193 --> 00:19:11.051
External scheduler (Kubernetes CronJob).

382
00:19:11.331 --> 00:19:11.993
How:

383
00:19:12.273 --> 00:19:13.937
Runs a separate job pod.

384
00:19:14.217 --> 00:19:14.835
When:

385
00:19:15.115 --> 00:19:17.505
Batch jobs that don't belong in the service.

386
00:19:18.855 --> 00:19:21.692
Whatever the mechanism, make jobs idempotent.

387
00:19:21.972 --> 00:19:29.332
Leases expire, and a slow or paused pod can still be running when another acquires the lock (fencing, Ch 20).

388
00:19:30.682 --> 00:19:32.261
Scaling reads and writes.

389
00:19:32.541 --> 00:19:33.885
Here is a quick table:

390
00:19:34.165 --> 00:19:36.149
To scale..., and In order of cost.

391
00:19:36.429 --> 00:19:37.218
Reads:

392
00:19:37.498 --> 00:19:50.511
Fix queries, then cache (Ch 07), then CDN, then read replicas (Ch 05), then read models or CQRS (Ch 15), then search engine (Ch 22).

393
00:19:50.791 --> 00:19:51.602
Writes:

394
00:19:51.882 --> 00:20:04.746
Fix queries and indexes, then batch and coalesce, then move non-critical writes async (queue), then vertical scale, then functional split (a DB per service), then partition or shard (Ch 11).

395
00:20:06.096 --> 00:20:08.976
Reads are easy to scale because copies are cheap.

396
00:20:09.256 --> 00:20:11.965
Writes are hard because every copy must agree.

397
00:20:12.245 --> 00:20:15.339
Most scaling work is moving writes off shared hot paths.

398
00:20:16.689 --> 00:20:18.758
Connection limits: the hidden ceiling.

399
00:20:19.038 --> 00:20:23.561
Adding pods multiplies every per-pod resource that points at something shared:

400
00:20:24.911 --> 00:20:26.255
Here is a quick table:

401
00:20:26.535 --> 00:20:29.927
Resource, and Limit, and What happens at scale-out, and Guard.

402
00:20:30.207 --> 00:20:31.487
DB connections.

403
00:20:31.767 --> 00:20:36.673
Limit: maxconnections, and far lower useful concurrency (Ch 05).

404
00:20:36.953 --> 00:20:38.340
What happens at scale-out:

405
00:20:38.620 --> 00:20:42.460
Pods times pool exceeds budget; queueing, context switching.

406
00:20:42.740 --> 00:20:43.487
Guard:

407
00:20:43.767 --> 00:20:48.332
Pool from Little's Law; PgBouncer; budget checks (Ch 02).

408
00:20:48.612 --> 00:20:49.807
Redis connections.

409
00:20:50.087 --> 00:20:53.564
Limit: maxclients (10 K default), per-shard CPU.

410
00:20:53.844 --> 00:20:55.231
What happens at scale-out:

411
00:20:55.511 --> 00:20:57.196
Connection storms on deploy.

412
00:20:57.476 --> 00:20:58.223
Guard:

413
00:20:58.503 --> 00:21:01.383
Shared multiplexed clients (Lettuce), limits.

414
00:21:01.663 --> 00:21:02.921
Kafka consumers.

415
00:21:03.201 --> 00:21:03.969
Limit:

416
00:21:04.249 --> 00:21:06.404
Consumers per group at most partitions.

417
00:21:06.684 --> 00:21:08.071
What happens at scale-out:

418
00:21:08.351 --> 00:21:10.761
Pods beyond the partition count sit idle.

419
00:21:11.041 --> 00:21:11.788
Guard:

420
00:21:12.068 --> 00:21:15.503
Partitions sized for max consumers (Ch 14).

421
00:21:15.783 --> 00:21:17.233
Third-party APIs.

422
00:21:17.513 --> 00:21:18.281
Limit:

423
00:21:18.561 --> 00:21:22.103
Razorpay, SMS or WhatsApp providers' rate limits.

424
00:21:22.383 --> 00:21:23.769
What happens at scale-out:

425
00:21:24.049 --> 00:21:27.804
More pods = more 429s, not more throughput.

426
00:21:28.084 --> 00:21:28.831
Guard:

427
00:21:29.111 --> 00:21:30.668
Central rate-limited queue.

428
00:21:32.018 --> 00:21:33.575
NAT gateway ports.

429
00:21:33.855 --> 00:21:34.623
Limit:

430
00:21:34.903 --> 00:21:37.890
Connections per destination (Ch 03).

431
00:21:38.170 --> 00:21:39.557
What happens at scale-out:

432
00:21:39.837 --> 00:21:41.778
Outbound failures to one partner.

433
00:21:42.058 --> 00:21:42.805
Guard:

434
00:21:43.085 --> 00:21:44.898
Pooling, more egress IPs.

435
00:21:45.178 --> 00:21:46.437
File descriptors.

436
00:21:46.717 --> 00:21:48.125
Limit: ulimit -n.

437
00:21:48.405 --> 00:21:49.791
What happens at scale-out:

438
00:21:50.071 --> 00:21:52.930
Streaming or pooled services hit FD limits.

439
00:21:53.210 --> 00:21:53.957
Guard:

440
00:21:54.237 --> 00:21:55.858
Raise limits, load test.

441
00:21:56.138 --> 00:21:57.461
LB or gateway.

442
00:21:57.741 --> 00:21:58.509
Limit:

443
00:21:58.789 --> 00:22:01.050
Connections, new connections per second.

444
00:22:01.330 --> 00:22:02.717
What happens at scale-out:

445
00:22:02.997 --> 00:22:05.727
Rejected connections during reconnect storms.

446
00:22:06.007 --> 00:22:06.754
Guard:

447
00:22:07.034 --> 00:22:09.445
Pre-scaling, jitter (Ch 03).

448
00:22:10.795 --> 00:22:11.712
Watch out:

449
00:22:11.992 --> 00:22:16.152
The fastest way to take down a database is to autoscale its clients.

450
00:22:16.432 --> 00:22:18.437
An HPA with maxReplicas:

451
00:22:18.717 --> 00:22:23.411
100 and a pool of 20 is a promise of up to 2,000 connections.

452
00:22:23.691 --> 00:22:28.683
Set maxReplicas from the connection budget and the USL peak, not from hope.

453
00:22:30.033 --> 00:22:31.889
The scale cube (X, Y, Z).

454
00:22:32.169 --> 00:22:33.513
Here is the diagram.

455
00:22:37.363 --> 00:22:38.707
Here is a quick table:

456
00:22:38.987 --> 00:22:42.656
Axis, and How, and Scales, and Costs, and Grocery example.

457
00:22:42.936 --> 00:22:44.877
X (horizontal duplication).

458
00:22:45.157 --> 00:22:45.819
How:

459
00:22:46.099 --> 00:22:47.635
Clone stateless instances.

460
00:22:47.915 --> 00:22:48.811
Scales:

461
00:22:49.091 --> 00:22:50.243
Request volume.

462
00:22:50.523 --> 00:22:51.333
Costs:

463
00:22:51.613 --> 00:22:53.363
Shared data stays a bottleneck.

464
00:22:53.643 --> 00:22:54.880
Grocery example:

465
00:22:55.160 --> 00:22:57.059
40 catalog-service pods.

466
00:22:57.339 --> 00:22:59.301
Y (functional decomposition).

467
00:22:59.581 --> 00:23:00.243
How:

468
00:23:00.523 --> 00:23:03.189
Split by capability or bounded context.

469
00:23:03.469 --> 00:23:04.365
Scales:

470
00:23:04.645 --> 00:23:08.016
Team autonomy, independent scaling, fault isolation.

471
00:23:08.296 --> 00:23:09.107
Costs:

472
00:23:09.387 --> 00:23:13.568
Distributed transactions, network calls (Ch 13, 16).

473
00:23:13.848 --> 00:23:17.709
Grocery example: cart, order, payment as separate services.

474
00:23:17.989 --> 00:23:19.525
Z (data partitioning).

475
00:23:19.805 --> 00:23:20.467
How:

476
00:23:20.747 --> 00:23:23.925
Each copy owns a subset of data, routed by a key.

477
00:23:24.205 --> 00:23:25.101
Scales:

478
00:23:25.381 --> 00:23:27.941
Data volume, writes, blast radius.

479
00:23:28.221 --> 00:23:29.032
Costs:

480
00:23:29.312 --> 00:23:33.088
Routing, rebalancing, cross-partition queries (Ch 11).

481
00:23:33.368 --> 00:23:34.605
Grocery example:

482
00:23:34.885 --> 00:23:39.963
Carts and inventory partitioned by city; cells per region (Ch 34).

483
00:23:41.313 --> 00:23:52.065
Choosing a Z-axis key for the grocery platform: city (or zone within a mega-city) is natural because orders, stores, riders and inventory rarely cross cities.

484
00:23:52.345 --> 00:23:56.441
It keeps most queries partition-local and limits a failure to one city.

485
00:23:56.721 --> 00:24:05.510
The risk is skew (Bengaluru is far bigger than Mysuru), handled by giving big cities their own partitions and packing small cities together.

486
00:24:06.860 --> 00:24:09.356
Elasticity: scaling on the right signal.

487
00:24:09.636 --> 00:24:10.980
Here is a quick table:

488
00:24:11.260 --> 00:24:13.287
Signal, and Good for, and Pitfall.

489
00:24:13.567 --> 00:24:14.505
CPU.

490
00:24:14.785 --> 00:24:15.617
Good for:

491
00:24:15.897 --> 00:24:17.540
CPU-bound services.

492
00:24:17.820 --> 00:24:18.652
Pitfall:

493
00:24:18.932 --> 00:24:24.201
I or O-bound services saturate on connections or latency at low CPU.

494
00:24:24.481 --> 00:24:26.401
In-flight requests or concurrency.

495
00:24:26.681 --> 00:24:27.513
Good for:

496
00:24:27.793 --> 00:24:29.756
Most request-response services.

497
00:24:30.036 --> 00:24:30.868
Pitfall:

498
00:24:31.148 --> 00:24:32.641
Needs a custom metric.

499
00:24:32.921 --> 00:24:34.585
Requests per second per pod.

500
00:24:34.865 --> 00:24:35.697
Good for:

501
00:24:35.977 --> 00:24:37.684
Predictable per-request cost.

502
00:24:37.964 --> 00:24:38.796
Pitfall:

503
00:24:39.076 --> 00:24:41.017
Doesn't reflect slow dependencies.

504
00:24:41.297 --> 00:24:43.473
Queue depth or consumer lag (KEDA).

505
00:24:43.753 --> 00:24:44.585
Good for:

506
00:24:44.865 --> 00:24:46.380
Workers and consumers.

507
00:24:46.660 --> 00:24:47.492
Pitfall:

508
00:24:47.772 --> 00:24:50.353
Consumers beyond the partition count do nothing.

509
00:24:50.633 --> 00:24:51.892
Schedule (cron).

510
00:24:52.172 --> 00:24:53.004
Good for:

511
00:24:53.284 --> 00:24:55.353
Known events (Ch 02).

512
00:24:55.633 --> 00:24:56.465
Pitfall:

513
00:24:56.745 --> 00:24:57.919
Needs a forecast.

514
00:24:59.269 --> 00:25:09.509
Autoscaling has reaction time (metric scrape, HPA sync, node provisioning, JVM warm-up), so it handles gradual growth and not step changes.

515
00:25:09.789 --> 00:25:20.861
Always pair it with a maxReplicas derived from connection budgets and the USL peak, and with admission control or load shedding for what can't be scaled in time (Ch 18).

516
00:25:22.211 --> 00:25:24.557
The 1, then 1 M users journey, then.

517
00:25:24.837 --> 00:25:28.229
Each stage is triggered by a measured limit, not by fashion.

518
00:25:28.509 --> 00:25:37.512
The numbers use the Ch 00 assumptions (~20 API calls per user per day on average; peak 5 times average).

519
00:25:38.862 --> 00:25:40.206
Here is a quick table:

520
00:25:40.486 --> 00:25:44.987
Stage, and Users (DAU), and Peak req per second, and What breaks, and Move.

