643
00:31:30.552 --> 00:31:32.557
Cart Service Scaling to 10 times.

644
00:31:33.807 --> 00:31:35.151
Here is a quick table:

645
00:31:35.431 --> 00:31:36.733
Field, and Value.

646
00:31:37.013 --> 00:31:38.357
Author or Reviewers:

647
00:31:38.637 --> 00:31:41.325
Cart team or Platform, SRE, Checkout.

648
00:31:41.605 --> 00:31:42.501
Status:

649
00:31:42.781 --> 00:31:43.655
Approved.

650
00:31:43.935 --> 00:31:45.258
Related ADRs:

651
00:31:45.538 --> 00:31:56.567
ADR-035 Carts as first-class Redis state, partitioned by city; ADR-036 maxReplicas from connection budget and USL fit.

652
00:31:57.917 --> 00:31:58.557
1.

653
00:31:58.837 --> 00:32:00.245
Context and Problem.

654
00:32:00.525 --> 00:32:13.325
Scaling cart-service from 20 to 64 pods reduced throughput (§1.1): a global config lock (σ), all-pod broadcast invalidation (κ), and a connection budget overrun.

655
00:32:13.605 --> 00:32:17.552
Carts lived in server sessions, requiring sticky routing for guests.

656
00:32:17.832 --> 00:32:18.536
2.

657
00:32:18.816 --> 00:32:20.160
Goals or Non-Goals.

658
00:32:20.440 --> 00:32:21.272
Goals:

659
00:32:21.552 --> 00:32:33.648
10 times cart throughput headroom over last festival; no scaling cliff below the planned max replicas; any pod serves any request; exactly-once guest merge; carts survive pod loss.

660
00:32:34.998 --> 00:32:44.278
Non-goals: checkout pricing (the SoR re-prices, Ch 07); cross-city carts (a customer's cart belongs to the delivery city).

661
00:32:44.558 --> 00:32:45.305
3.

662
00:32:45.585 --> 00:32:46.545
Requirements.

663
00:32:47.895 --> 00:32:49.239
Here is a quick table:

664
00:32:49.519 --> 00:32:50.991
ID, and Requirement.

665
00:32:51.271 --> 00:32:52.209
FR-1:

666
00:32:52.489 --> 00:32:58.036
Add, update, remove cart lines; view cart; merge guest into customer cart on login.

667
00:32:58.316 --> 00:32:59.361
NFR-1:

668
00:32:59.641 --> 00:33:06.297
60 K cart ops per second at P90 sale peak, p99 at most 30 ms in-service.

669
00:33:06.577 --> 00:33:07.623
NFR-2:

670
00:33:07.903 --> 00:33:12.681
USL-fitted peak N ≥ 2 times planned max replicas.

671
00:33:12.961 --> 00:33:18.807
NFR-3: Σ (pods times pool) at most connection budget for every shared store.

672
00:33:19.087 --> 00:33:20.175
NFR-4:

673
00:33:20.455 --> 00:33:24.017
Merge is idempotent and atomic under concurrent requests.

674
00:33:24.297 --> 00:33:25.343
NFR-5:

675
00:33:25.623 --> 00:33:32.300
Losing any pod or zone loses no cart state; losing a Redis shard affects one partition only.

676
00:33:33.650 --> 00:33:34.354
4.

677
00:33:34.634 --> 00:33:37.151
Capacity estimates and scaling analysis.

678
00:33:38.501 --> 00:33:39.845
Here is a quick table:

679
00:33:40.125 --> 00:33:42.429
Item, and Calculation, and Result.

680
00:33:42.709 --> 00:33:44.736
Cart ops at P90 sale peak.

681
00:33:45.016 --> 00:33:46.083
Calculation:

682
00:33:46.363 --> 00:33:53.637
Ch 02: ~14.5 K cart req per second times ~4 Redis ops per request.

683
00:33:53.917 --> 00:33:56.840
Result: ~60 K Redis ops per second.

684
00:33:57.120 --> 00:33:58.165
Carts in Redis.

685
00:33:58.445 --> 00:33:59.512
Calculation:

686
00:33:59.792 --> 00:34:03.035
1.5 M active times ~1 KB.

687
00:34:03.315 --> 00:34:06.771
Result: ~1.5 GB (+ replicas).

688
00:34:07.051 --> 00:34:09.568
Load test (pods, then req per second).

689
00:34:09.848 --> 00:34:10.915
Calculation:

690
00:34:11.195 --> 00:34:13.840
1, then 1,470...

691
00:34:14.120 --> 00:34:16.851
24, then 17,800.

692
00:34:17.131 --> 00:34:26.581
Result: σ about 0.030, κ about 0.00052, N about 43 (L1 fit).

693
00:34:26.861 --> 00:34:29.699
Planned peak pods (Ch 02 method).

694
00:34:29.979 --> 00:34:31.045
Calculation:

695
00:34:31.325 --> 00:34:39.176
14.5 K req per second ÷ ~1.2 K per pod times 1.5 AZ headroom.

696
00:34:39.456 --> 00:34:42.059
Result: ~18 to 24 pods.

697
00:34:42.339 --> 00:34:44.280
NFR-2 check before fixes.

698
00:34:44.560 --> 00:34:45.627
Calculation:

699
00:34:45.907 --> 00:34:49.448
N 43 ≥ 2 times 24?

700
00:34:49.728 --> 00:34:50.539
Result:

701
00:34:50.819 --> 00:34:52.696
Fails (43 48).

702
00:34:52.976 --> 00:35:02.235
After removing the config lock (σ, then ~0.01) and broadcasts (κ, then ~0.0001).

703
00:35:02.515 --> 00:35:04.179
Calculation: re-test.

704
00:35:04.459 --> 00:35:05.269
Result:

705
00:35:05.549 --> 00:35:09.624
Target N ≥ 90 (verified by load test).

706
00:35:10.974 --> 00:35:13.235
DB connection budget (snapshots).

707
00:35:13.515 --> 00:35:14.582
Calculation:

708
00:35:14.862 --> 00:35:19.427
Snapshot writes ~100 per second times 20 ms about 2 busy.

709
00:35:19.707 --> 00:35:20.518
Result:

710
00:35:20.798 --> 00:35:24.147
Pool of 2 per pod, then 48 at 24 pods.

711
00:35:24.427 --> 00:35:25.622
Redis connections.

712
00:35:25.902 --> 00:35:26.969
Calculation:

713
00:35:27.249 --> 00:35:28.081
Lettuce:

714
00:35:28.361 --> 00:35:31.006
1 multiplexed connection per shard per pod.

715
00:35:31.286 --> 00:35:32.097
Result:

716
00:35:32.377 --> 00:35:35.769
24 pods times 3 shards = 72.

717
00:35:37.119 --> 00:35:38.036
Success:

718
00:35:38.316 --> 00:35:39.895
What the analysis tells us.

719
00:35:40.175 --> 00:35:42.073
The fleet was never short of pods.

720
00:35:42.353 --> 00:35:43.996
It was short of independence.

721
00:35:44.276 --> 00:35:53.279
Fixing σ and κ roughly doubles the useful pod count and raises the throughput ceiling, while adding pods to the old design would have lowered it.

722
00:35:54.629 --> 00:35:55.397
5.

723
00:35:55.677 --> 00:35:56.871
Proposed design.

724
00:35:57.151 --> 00:35:58.325
Stateless pods.

725
00:35:58.605 --> 00:36:01.079
Identity from the gateway JWT.

726
00:36:01.359 --> 00:36:07.589
Guest carts are identified by a server-issued, HMAC-signed guest cart id (no server session).

727
00:36:07.869 --> 00:36:09.170
No sticky sessions.

728
00:36:09.450 --> 00:36:11.349
Carts are first-class Redis state.

729
00:36:11.629 --> 00:36:22.167
One HASH per cart (sku, then qty), with atomic Lua scripts for set-quantity (with max lines and max quantity per line) and merge (with an idempotency marker).

730
00:36:22.447 --> 00:36:24.090
TTL refreshed on write.

731
00:36:24.370 --> 00:36:28.935
A snapshot job writes changed carts to PostgreSQL every 5 minutes.

732
00:36:30.285 --> 00:36:32.269
Remove σ and κ sources.

733
00:36:32.549 --> 00:36:42.064
Cart limits come from immutable config loaded at startup and refreshed through a versioned snapshot (the Ch 01 pattern), with no per-request lock.

734
00:36:42.344 --> 00:36:50.429
Promotion changes are published once to a Kafka topic, and pods read it (one write, N reads) instead of pod-to-pod broadcasts.

735
00:36:50.709 --> 00:36:52.053
Z-axis by city.

736
00:36:52.333 --> 00:36:57.027
A CityShardRouter maps city to Redis shard via a versioned shard map.

737
00:36:57.307 --> 00:37:01.616
Bengaluru and Mumbai get dedicated shards, and small cities share one.

738
00:37:01.896 --> 00:37:06.184
A city can move between shards online (lazy copy-on-read, L4).

739
00:37:07.534 --> 00:37:08.878
Scale-safe jobs.

740
00:37:09.158 --> 00:37:17.286
The abandoned-cart reminder runs once per interval under a Redis job lock with a lease, and it's idempotent (a reminder marker per cart).

741
00:37:17.566 --> 00:37:19.038
Elasticity guardrails.

742
00:37:19.318 --> 00:37:26.486
HPA on in-flight requests per pod. maxReplicas = min(connection-budget limit, N/2).

743
00:37:26.766 --> 00:37:27.513
7.

744
00:37:27.793 --> 00:37:29.307
Alternatives considered.

745
00:37:30.657 --> 00:37:32.001
Here is a quick table:

746
00:37:32.281 --> 00:37:34.820
Option, and Pros, and Cons, and Verdict.

747
00:37:35.100 --> 00:37:38.044
Keep carts in server sessions, add sticky sessions.

748
00:37:38.324 --> 00:37:39.177
Pros:

749
00:37:39.457 --> 00:37:40.673
No code change.

750
00:37:40.953 --> 00:37:41.764
Cons:

751
00:37:42.044 --> 00:37:46.161
Uneven load; carts lost on deploy; session blob contention.

752
00:37:46.441 --> 00:37:47.252
Verdict:

753
00:37:47.532 --> 00:37:48.449
Rejected.

754
00:37:48.729 --> 00:37:51.289
Spring Session (Redis) with cart in the session.

755
00:37:51.569 --> 00:37:52.423
Pros:

756
00:37:52.703 --> 00:37:54.153
Stateless pods quickly.

757
00:37:54.433 --> 00:37:55.244
Cons:

758
00:37:55.524 --> 00:38:00.089
Whole-session rewrites; last-write-wins across tabs; no atomic merge.

759
00:38:00.369 --> 00:38:01.180
Verdict:

760
00:38:01.460 --> 00:38:04.511
Interim step (L2), rejected as the end state.

761
00:38:04.791 --> 00:38:06.839
Carts in PostgreSQL only.

762
00:38:07.119 --> 00:38:07.972
Pros:

763
00:38:08.252 --> 00:38:09.831
One store, transactional.

764
00:38:10.111 --> 00:38:10.921
Cons:

765
00:38:11.201 --> 00:38:14.999
60 K ops per second of small writes on the orders cluster.

766
00:38:15.279 --> 00:38:16.089
Verdict:

767
00:38:16.369 --> 00:38:18.268
Rejected (snapshot only).

768
00:38:18.548 --> 00:38:23.348
Carts as Redis HASHes + Lua + city partitioning + DB snapshots.

769
00:38:23.628 --> 00:38:24.481
Pros:

770
00:38:24.761 --> 00:38:28.196
Atomic ops, fast, partitionable, survives pod loss.

771
00:38:28.476 --> 00:38:29.287
Cons:

772
00:38:29.567 --> 00:38:33.940
Redis is a critical store: needs replicas, a snapshot for recovery.

773
00:38:34.220 --> 00:38:35.031
Verdict:

774
00:38:35.311 --> 00:38:36.143
Chosen.

775
00:38:37.493 --> 00:38:39.157
Just raise maxReplicas.

776
00:38:39.437 --> 00:38:40.290
Pros:

777
00:38:40.570 --> 00:38:41.381
Instant.

778
00:38:41.661 --> 00:38:42.471
Cons:

779
00:38:42.751 --> 00:38:45.567
Retrograde scaling (§1.1).

780
00:38:45.847 --> 00:38:46.658
Verdict:

781
00:38:46.938 --> 00:38:47.855
Rejected.

782
00:38:49.205 --> 00:38:49.888
8.

783
00:38:50.168 --> 00:38:51.320
Failure modes.

