01:01:02.187 --> 01:01:07.350
And a deterministic negative control, where a stale pod writes after the sweep has finished:

1079
01:01:08.700 --> 01:01:24.743
On screen: without fence: stale-pod writes lost = 100 or 100, orphan carts left on old shard = 100. with fence: stale-pod writes lost = 0 or 100, orphan carts left on old shard = 0.

1080
01:01:27.593 --> 01:01:36.126
The fence is what makes the move safe: without it, every write from a pod with a stale map lands on the old shard and is silently orphaned.

1081
01:01:36.406 --> 01:01:57.057
The same interleavings were checked against a real Redis 7.0 server with redis-cli --eval: a stale write to the fenced shard returns -3, and when two movers race while a pod updates the new shard, RICE (only on the old shard) survives, MILK keeps its newer value 5, and EGGS (only on the new shard) is kept.

1082
01:01:58.407 --> 01:01:59.153
Warning:

1083
01:01:59.433 --> 01:02:00.948
One accepted anomaly.

1084
01:02:01.228 --> 01:02:14.220
If a customer removes a line on the new shard in the milliseconds between another pod's EXPORT and IMPORTMERGE of the same cart, the import re-adds that line (the new shard no longer has it, so HSETNX writes it).

1085
01:02:14.500 --> 01:02:21.519
For carts this is harmless (the customer sees the item and removes it again) and far cheaper than per-line tombstones.

1086
01:02:21.799 --> 01:02:31.975
For data where a resurrected delete matters (money, entitlements), don't use lazy moves: use a write freeze per key range, or move through the log (Ch 11).

1087
01:02:33.325 --> 01:02:34.071
