01:09:09.537 --> 01:09:13.803
Stabilization windows; scale on concurrency, not raw CPU.

1247
01:09:14.083 --> 01:09:16.003
Scale-in drops in-flight requests.

1248
01:09:16.283 --> 01:09:17.478
Detection signal:

1249
01:09:17.758 --> 01:09:20.233
5xx spikes when replicas decrease.

1250
01:09:20.513 --> 01:09:21.729
Blast radius:

1251
01:09:22.009 --> 01:09:23.331
Users mid-request.

1252
01:09:23.611 --> 01:09:24.614
Mitigation:

1253
01:09:24.894 --> 01:09:29.182
Graceful shutdown, preStop delay, readiness false before termination.

1254
01:09:30.532 --> 01:09:31.620
Observability.

1255
01:09:31.900 --> 01:09:33.244
Here is a quick table:

1256
01:09:33.524 --> 01:09:34.655
Signal, and Why.

1257
01:09:34.935 --> 01:09:38.497
Throughput per pod versus pod count (a live USL view):

1258
01:09:38.777 --> 01:09:41.273
The earliest sign of σ or κ growth.

1259
01:09:41.553 --> 01:09:45.713
Lock wait time, time in serialized sections: σ sources.

1260
01:09:45.993 --> 01:09:51.817
Pod-to-pod message or invalidation rate: κ sources (should be O(N), not O(N²)).

1261
01:09:52.097 --> 01:09:55.532
Connections per store versus budget (per service and total):

1262
01:09:55.812 --> 01:09:56.879
The hidden ceiling.

1263
01:09:57.159 --> 01:10:01.745
In-flight requests per pod (the HPA signal) and HPA decisions:

1264
01:10:02.025 --> 01:10:03.220
Elasticity health.

1265
01:10:03.500 --> 01:10:05.484
Consumer lag versus replicas:

1266
01:10:05.764 --> 01:10:07.919
Whether scaling out helps a consumer.

1267
01:10:08.199 --> 01:10:11.719
Per-shard ops per second, memory, CPU; carts per city:

1268
01:10:11.999 --> 01:10:14.025
Z-axis balance and hot spots.

1269
01:10:15.375 --> 01:10:20.047
cart.mergeoutcome, cart.shard.movedredirects, shard map version per pod:

1270
01:10:20.327 --> 01:10:22.802
Correctness of externalized state and moves.

1271
01:10:23.082 --> 01:10:26.133
Job lock acquisitions and skipped runs per job:

1272
01:10:26.413 --> 01:10:27.757
Scale-safe jobs.

1273
01:10:29.107 --> 01:10:30.045
Security.

1274
01:10:30.325 --> 01:10:32.331
Signed identifiers for guest state.

1275
01:10:32.611 --> 01:10:40.333
Guest cart ids are HMAC-signed and include the city, so clients can't read or modify other carts by guessing ids.

1276
01:10:40.613 --> 01:10:43.152
Session cookies (if you use server sessions):

1277
01:10:43.432 --> 01:10:48.765
Secure, HttpOnly, SameSite, rotated on login (session fixation).

1278
01:10:49.045 --> 01:10:51.477
Spring Session supports id rotation.

1279
01:10:51.757 --> 01:11:02.467
Shared stores are shared attack surface: a Redis holding every customer's cart needs ACLs per service, TLS, and no public exposure (Ch 06).

1280
01:11:02.747 --> 01:11:08.805
Per-tenant or per-city partitioning limits blast radius for data exposure as well as outages.

1281
01:11:10.155 --> 01:11:15.297
Autoscaling can amplify abuse: a bot flood scales you out and raises your bill.

1282
01:11:15.577 --> 01:11:20.825
Rate limits at the edge (Ch 08) must act before the autoscaler does.

1283
01:11:22.175 --> 01:11:23.839
Performance and tuning knobs.

1284
01:11:24.119 --> 01:11:25.463
Here is a quick table:

1285
01:11:25.743 --> 01:11:29.241
Knob, and Starting point, and Guidance. maxReplicas.

1286
01:11:29.521 --> 01:11:34.023
Starting point: min(connection budget ÷ pool, N/2).

1287
01:11:34.303 --> 01:11:35.199
Guidance:

1288
01:11:35.479 --> 01:11:37.505
Recompute after each load test.

1289
01:11:37.785 --> 01:11:39.641
HPA or KEDA target.

1290
01:11:39.921 --> 01:11:40.988
Starting point:

1291
01:11:41.268 --> 01:11:45.257
In-flight about Little's Law per pod at 60 % utilization.

1292
01:11:45.537 --> 01:11:46.433
Guidance:

1293
01:11:46.713 --> 01:11:50.383
Concurrency beats CPU for I or O-bound services.

1294
01:11:50.663 --> 01:11:53.009
Scale-up or scale-down stabilization.

1295
01:11:53.289 --> 01:11:54.356
Starting point:

1296
01:11:54.636 --> 01:11:57.132
0 to 30 s up, 5 min down.

1297
01:11:57.412 --> 01:11:58.308
Guidance:

1298
01:11:58.588 --> 01:12:00.508
Avoid flapping and cold caches.

1299
01:12:00.788 --> 01:12:01.876
Pool sizes.

1300
01:12:02.156 --> 01:12:03.223
Starting point:

1301
01:12:03.503 --> 01:12:04.804
Little's Law per pod.

1302
01:12:05.084 --> 01:12:05.980
Guidance:

1303
01:12:06.260 --> 01:12:08.628
Small for secondary stores (snapshots:

1304
01:12:08.908 --> 01:12:09.633
2).

1305
01:12:09.913 --> 01:12:10.959
Redis client.

1306
01:12:11.239 --> 01:12:12.305
Starting point:

1307
01:12:12.585 --> 01:12:15.785
One multiplexed Lettuce connection per shard per pod.

1308
01:12:16.065 --> 01:12:16.961
Guidance:

1309
01:12:17.241 --> 01:12:18.777
Avoids connection storms.

1310
01:12:19.057 --> 01:12:20.295
Graceful shutdown.

1311
01:12:20.575 --> 01:12:21.641
Starting point:

1312
01:12:21.921 --> 01:12:25.164
20 to 30 s + preStop 5 to 10 s.

1313
01:12:25.444 --> 01:12:26.340
Guidance:

1314
01:12:26.620 --> 01:12:29.073
Longer for streams (Ch 03).

1315
01:12:30.423 --> 01:12:31.533
Cart TTL.

1316
01:12:31.813 --> 01:12:32.879
Starting point:

1317
01:12:33.159 --> 01:12:35.442
7 days (guest), refreshed on write.

1318
01:12:35.722 --> 01:12:36.618
Guidance:

1319
01:12:36.898 --> 01:12:37.943
Bounds memory.

1320
01:12:38.223 --> 01:12:39.226
Job lease.

1321
01:12:39.506 --> 01:12:43.794
Starting point: p99 job duration; idempotent work.

1322
01:12:44.074 --> 01:12:44.970
Guidance:

1323
01:12:45.250 --> 01:12:47.447
Fencing where side effects are external.

1324
01:12:47.727 --> 01:12:49.093
Shards per big city.

1325
01:12:49.373 --> 01:12:50.439
Starting point:

1326
01:12:50.719 --> 01:12:55.925
1 dedicated, split by zone above ~50 % of one shard's capacity.

1327
01:12:56.205 --> 01:12:57.101
Guidance:

1328
01:12:57.381 --> 01:12:59.023
Keep headroom for sale peaks.

1329
01:13:00.373 --> 01:13:01.099
Cost.

1330
01:13:01.379 --> 01:13:06.115
Horizontal scaling isn't free: past the USL peak you pay more for less.

1331
01:13:06.395 --> 01:13:10.064
The cheapest capacity is removing a σ or κ source.

1332
01:13:10.344 --> 01:13:16.403
Vertical scaling of stateful stores is often cheaper than sharding for the next 12 to 18 months.

1333
01:13:16.683 --> 01:13:17.685
Price both.

1334
01:13:17.965 --> 01:13:24.323
Z-axis partitions add fixed cost (a Redis deployment per big city, with replicas and monitoring).

1335
01:13:24.603 --> 01:13:26.032
Pack small cities together.

1336
01:13:26.312 --> 01:13:34.461
Autoscaling saves money only if scale-in works: graceful shutdown, no local state, and caches that tolerate cold starts.

1337
01:13:35.811 --> 01:13:42.318
Per-pod overheads multiply: sidecars, agents, JVM heap baselines and connection pools.

1338
01:13:42.598 --> 01:13:47.675
A few larger pods can be cheaper than many small ones when per-pod overhead dominates.

1339
01:13:49.025 --> 01:13:49.964
Part 7.

1340
01:13:50.244 --> 01:13:51.951
Anti-Patterns and Pitfalls.

1341
01:13:53.201 --> 01:13:53.841
1.

1342
01:13:54.121 --> 01:13:55.273
Just add pods.

1343
01:13:55.553 --> 01:13:56.363
Symptom:

1344
01:13:56.643 --> 01:14:00.185
Throughput falls as replicas rise (§1.1).

1345
01:14:00.465 --> 01:14:01.190
Fix:

1346
01:14:01.470 --> 01:14:08.809
Load test at several pod counts, fit the USL, remove the σ or κ sources, and cap maxReplicas.

1347
01:14:09.089 --> 01:14:09.793
2.

1348
01:14:10.073 --> 01:14:11.907
Global locks on the request path.

1349
01:14:13.257 --> 01:14:29.001
Here is the Java code. before: every add-to-cart serializes on one Redis key across the fleet (σ). after: immutable, versioned config loaded at startup or refresh (Ch 01 snapshot pattern); no request-time lock.

1350
01:14:31.851 --> 01:14:32.598
3.

1351
01:14:32.878 --> 01:14:34.307
All-to-all broadcasts.

1352
01:14:34.587 --> 01:14:35.398
Symptom:

1353
01:14:35.678 --> 01:14:40.883
Message volume grows with N² and pods spend CPU on coordination (κ).

1354
01:14:41.163 --> 01:14:41.889
Fix:

1355
01:14:42.169 --> 01:14:48.185
Publish once to a log or topic that every pod reads (O(N)), or use an external shared cache.

1356
01:14:48.465 --> 01:14:49.169
4.

1357
01:14:49.449 --> 01:14:52.947
Sessions in pod memory, and sticky sessions to compensate.

1358
01:14:53.227 --> 01:14:54.038
Symptom:

1359
01:14:54.318 --> 01:14:57.027
Deploys log users out, and load is uneven.

1360
01:14:57.307 --> 01:14:58.033
Fix:

1361
01:14:58.313 --> 01:14:59.486
Stateless pods.

1362
01:14:59.766 --> 01:15:04.801
Tokens for identity, Redis for session-like state, first-class storage for carts.

1363
01:15:05.081 --> 01:15:05.849
5.

1364
01:15:06.129 --> 01:15:08.902
Storing growing domain state inside the session.

1365
01:15:10.252 --> 01:15:20.513
Here is the Java code. before: whole cart rewritten on every change; two tabs overwrite each other. after: per-line atomic update on a first-class key.

1366
01:15:23.363 --> 01:15:25.753
6. @Scheduled jobs without a lock.

1367
01:15:26.033 --> 01:15:26.843
Symptom:

1368
01:15:27.123 --> 01:15:29.897
40 pods send 40 abandoned-cart reminders.

1369
01:15:30.177 --> 01:15:30.902
Fix:

1370
01:15:31.182 --> 01:15:34.915
A job lock with a lease and owner token, plus idempotent work.

1371
01:15:35.195 --> 01:15:35.942
7.

1372
01:15:36.222 --> 01:15:39.443
HPA maxReplicas without a connection budget.

1373
01:15:40.793 --> 01:15:42.265
Here is the YAML code.

1374
01:15:45.115 --> 01:15:45.798
8.

1375
01:15:46.078 --> 01:15:48.489
Scaling consumers past the partition count.

1376
01:15:48.769 --> 01:15:49.579
Symptom:

1377
01:15:49.859 --> 01:15:54.766
48 consumer pods on a 24-partition topic, and half of them idle.

1378
01:15:55.046 --> 01:15:55.771
Fix:

1379
01:15:56.051 --> 01:16:02.771
Plan partitions for peak consumers (Ch 14), and scale on lag with a cap at the partition count.

1380
01:16:03.051 --> 01:16:03.798
9.

1381
01:16:04.078 --> 01:16:06.019
Moving partitions without fencing.

1382
01:16:06.299 --> 01:16:07.110
Symptom:

1383
01:16:07.390 --> 01:16:16.350
Writes from pods with a stale shard map land on the old shard and are orphaned (the L4 negative control loses 100 or 100).

1384
01:16:16.630 --> 01:16:17.355
Fix:

1385
01:16:17.635 --> 01:16:23.054
Fence the old location for the moving range, redirect with MOVED, and sweep until empty.

1386
01:16:24.404 --> 01:16:25.108
10.

1387
01:16:25.388 --> 01:16:26.561
Premature sharding.

1388
01:16:26.841 --> 01:16:27.652
Symptom:

1389
01:16:27.932 --> 01:16:35.100
A Z-axis design for 50 K users, with cross-shard queries everywhere and a team busy operating shards.

1390
01:16:35.380 --> 01:16:36.105
Fix:

1391
01:16:36.385 --> 01:16:40.673
Climb the journey (§2.12) only when measurements say so.

1392
01:16:40.953 --> 01:16:42.681
Scale up the database first.

1393
01:16:44.031 --> 01:16:44.906
Part 8.

1394
01:16:45.186 --> 01:16:46.530
Interview Q and A.

1395
01:16:47.780 --> 01:16:48.612
Beginner.

1396
01:16:48.892 --> 01:16:49.916
Question 1.

1397
01:16:50.196 --> 01:16:55.273
Vertical versus horizontal scaling: what's the difference, and when would you choose each?

1398
01:16:55.553 --> 01:16:56.919
Thirty-second answer:

1399
01:16:57.199 --> 01:17:04.964
Vertical means a bigger machine: no code change, but it has a ceiling, a single failure domain, and superlinear cost at the top.

1400
01:17:05.244 --> 01:17:15.633
Horizontal means more machines: near-linear capacity and better fault isolation, but it needs stateless services or partitioned state and brings coordination costs.

1401
01:17:15.913 --> 01:17:23.060
Scale stateless tiers horizontally from day one, and scale databases vertically until measurements say you must partition.

1402
01:17:24.410 --> 01:17:25.413
Question 2.

1403
01:17:25.693 --> 01:17:29.063
What does 'stateless service' mean, and why does it matter for scaling?

1404
01:17:29.343 --> 01:17:30.709
Thirty-second answer:

1405
01:17:30.989 --> 01:17:42.743
Any instance can serve any request, and losing an instance loses only in-flight work, because state lives in stores designed for it (a database, Redis, object storage, tokens).

1406
01:17:43.023 --> 01:17:49.146
That enables load balancing without stickiness, autoscaling, rolling deploys and failure recovery.

1407
01:17:50.496 --> 01:17:51.541
Question 3.

1408
01:17:51.821 --> 01:17:55.427
Where should user sessions live in a horizontally scaled system?

1409
01:17:55.707 --> 01:17:57.072
Thirty-second answer:

1410
01:17:57.352 --> 01:17:58.696
Not in pod memory.

1411
01:17:58.976 --> 01:18:09.109
Use signed tokens (JWT) for identity in APIs, and an external session store (Redis via Spring Session) when you need server-side session data.

1412
01:18:09.389 --> 01:18:10.776
Avoid sticky sessions.

1413
01:18:12.126 --> 01:18:13.193
Question 4.

1414
01:18:13.473 --> 01:18:14.817
What is the scale cube?

1415
01:18:15.097 --> 01:18:16.462
Thirty-second answer:

1416
01:18:16.742 --> 01:18:17.979
Three ways to scale:

1417
01:18:18.259 --> 01:18:31.785
X-axis (clone stateless instances), Y-axis (split by function into services), and Z-axis (split data by a key such as customer, city or tenant, with each partition served by the same code).

1418
01:18:32.065 --> 01:18:34.070
Real platforms combine all three.

1419
01:18:35.420 --> 01:18:36.423
Intermediate.

1420
01:18:36.703 --> 01:18:37.812
Question 5.

1421
01:18:38.092 --> 01:18:42.529
Walk me through scaling a grocery app from 1 K to 1 M daily users.

1422
01:18:42.809 --> 01:18:44.175
Thirty-second answer:

1423
01:18:44.455 --> 01:19:05.980
One box, then a separate managed database, then stateless app instances behind a load balancer with sessions and uploads externalized, then caching and a CDN, then queues for async work and read replicas, then splitting hot capabilities (search, tracking, payments), then multi-AZ with pre-scaling and city partitioning for hot write paths.

1424
01:19:06.260 --> 01:19:08.393
Each step is triggered by a measured limit.

1425
01:19:09.743 --> 01:19:10.853
Question 6.

1426
01:19:11.133 --> 01:19:15.293
How do you make scheduled jobs safe when a service runs on 40 pods?

1427
01:19:15.573 --> 01:19:16.938
Thirty-second answer:

1428
01:19:17.218 --> 01:19:31.981
Ensure one execution per interval with a job lock (ShedLock, Redis SET NX PX with an owner token, or a DB lock) or leader election, and make the work idempotent, because leases can expire while a pod is paused.

1429
01:19:32.261 --> 01:19:39.151
For large jobs, partition the work so every pod takes a disjoint slice (SKIP LOCKED, Kafka partitions).

1430
01:19:40.501 --> 01:19:41.589
Question 7.

1431
01:19:41.869 --> 01:19:44.429
What hidden limits appear when you scale out a service?

1432
01:19:44.709 --> 01:19:46.075
Thirty-second answer:

1433
01:19:46.355 --> 01:20:02.440
Database connections (pods times pool), Redis connections, Kafka consumers per group ( at most partitions), third-party API rate limits, NAT ports, file descriptors, LB connection limits, license limits, and shared locks or hot keys.

1434
01:20:02.720 --> 01:20:07.221
Adding pods multiplies every per-pod resource that points at something shared.

1435
01:20:08.571 --> 01:20:09.595
Question 8.

1436
01:20:09.875 --> 01:20:13.907
Why is storing a cart in the HTTP session a problem at scale?

1437
01:20:14.187 --> 01:20:15.553
Thirty-second answer:

1438
01:20:15.833 --> 01:20:24.025
The whole session is rewritten on each change, so concurrent updates from two tabs or devices overwrite each other (last write wins).

1439
01:20:24.305 --> 01:20:34.054
It can't be merged atomically on login, its lifetime is tied to the session, it can't be queried (abandoned-cart jobs), and it can't be partitioned meaningfully.

1440
01:20:35.404 --> 01:20:36.471
Question 9.

1441
01:20:36.751 --> 01:20:38.649
What should an autoscaler scale on?

1442
01:20:38.929 --> 01:20:40.295
Thirty-second answer:

1443
01:20:40.575 --> 01:20:54.847
A signal that tracks saturation for that workload: in-flight requests or concurrency for request or response services, queue depth or lag for consumers, CPU only when CPU-bound, and schedules for known events.

1444
01:20:55.127 --> 01:20:59.201
Cap with maxReplicas from budgets, and use stabilization windows.

1445
01:21:00.551 --> 01:21:01.319
Senior.

1446
01:21:01.599 --> 01:21:02.645
Question 10.

1447
01:21:02.925 --> 01:21:05.186
We doubled the pods and throughput went down.

1448
01:21:05.466 --> 01:21:06.703
Explain and fix it.

1449
01:21:06.983 --> 01:21:08.349
Thirty-second answer:

1450
01:21:08.629 --> 01:21:12.533
Retrograde scaling: shared costs grew faster than capacity.

1451
01:21:12.813 --> 01:21:17.442
Contention (σ): a global lock, a hot key, one database primary.

1452
01:21:17.722 --> 01:21:23.418
Coherency (κ): all-to-all broadcasts, cluster membership chatter, cross-shard coordination.

1453
01:21:23.698 --> 01:21:25.277
Plus connection overruns.

1454
01:21:25.557 --> 01:21:26.986
The USL models it:

1455
01:21:27.266 --> 01:21:36.311
C(N) = N / (1 + σ(Nminus 1) + κN(Nminus 1)) peaks at √((1minus σ)/κ).

1456
01:21:36.591 --> 01:21:38.895
Fix the sources, then cap replicas.

1457
01:21:40.245 --> 01:21:41.376
Question 11.

1458
01:21:41.656 --> 01:21:45.496
Explain Amdahl's law versus the Universal Scalability Law.

1459
01:21:45.776 --> 01:21:47.141
Thirty-second answer:

1460
01:21:47.421 --> 01:21:52.520
Amdahl: a serial fraction s caps speed-up at 1 per second, and throughput plateaus.

1461
01:21:52.800 --> 01:22:00.373
The USL adds a coherency term κ for pairwise coordination, which makes throughput peak and then fall as N grows.

1462
01:22:00.653 --> 01:22:02.317
Amdahl explains plateaus.

1463
01:22:02.597 --> 01:22:06.416
The USL explains why adding capacity can make things worse.

1464
01:22:07.766 --> 01:22:08.854
Question 12.

1465
01:22:09.134 --> 01:22:13.507
Design exactly-once guest-to-customer cart merge across many pods.

1466
01:22:13.787 --> 01:22:15.153
Thirty-second answer:

1467
01:22:15.433 --> 01:22:30.323
Store both carts under the same hash tag (same city slot) in Redis and run one Lua script that checks a merge marker, merges lines with limits (capped quantities, max lines), deletes the guest cart, and sets the marker, all atomically.

1468
01:22:30.603 --> 01:22:34.102
Retries on any pod see the marker and return the current cart.

1469
01:22:35.452 --> 01:22:36.732
Question 13.

1470
01:22:37.012 --> 01:22:40.169
How do you move data between partitions without downtime?

1471
01:22:40.449 --> 01:22:41.815
Thirty-second answer:

1472
01:22:42.095 --> 01:22:43.503
Version the routing map.

1473
01:22:43.783 --> 01:22:47.025
Mark the range as moving (new primary, old fallback).

1474
01:22:47.305 --> 01:22:50.911
Fence the old location so stale writers get redirected.

1475
01:22:51.191 --> 01:22:59.532
Move lazily on access (atomic export from old, merge-if-absent import on new), sweep the rest, then publish the final map.

1476
01:23:00.882 --> 01:23:02.077
Question 14.

1477
01:23:02.357 --> 01:23:04.725
How do you prove a service is really stateless?

1478
01:23:05.005 --> 01:23:06.370
Thirty-second answer:

1479
01:23:06.650 --> 01:23:23.674
Review the hidden-state checklist, then test it: run many replicas behind round-robin, kill random pods during a load test (and during rolling deploys and scale-in), and assert there are no lost sessions, carts or duplicate jobs, and no errors beyond in-flight requests.

1480
01:23:23.954 --> 01:23:28.946
Also run two instances in integration tests sharing only external stores.

1481
01:23:30.296 --> 01:23:31.192
Architect.

1482
01:23:31.472 --> 01:23:32.667
Question 15.

1483
01:23:32.947 --> 01:23:37.128
How do you choose a partition (Z-axis) key for a grocery platform?

1484
01:23:37.408 --> 01:23:38.773
Thirty-second answer:

1485
01:23:39.053 --> 01:23:52.664
Choose the key that keeps most operations inside one partition and spreads load: city (or zone within big cities) for carts, inventory, orders-in-flight, riders and stores, because they rarely cross cities.

1486
01:23:52.944 --> 01:24:00.069
Check skew (Bengaluru versus Mysuru), and give big cities dedicated partitions while packing small ones together.

1487
01:24:00.349 --> 01:24:03.400
Customer-level views across cities go to read models.

1488
01:24:04.750 --> 01:24:05.945
Question 16.

1489
01:24:06.225 --> 01:24:07.654
When would you not scale out?

1490
01:24:07.934 --> 01:24:09.299
Thirty-second answer:

1491
01:24:09.579 --> 01:24:28.993
When a bigger instance solves the next 12 to 18 months more cheaply; when the bottleneck is shared (adding pods adds contention); when the problem is a slow query or missing index; when the load is a known spike better handled by pre-scaling or shedding; and when operational maturity isn't ready for more moving parts.

1492
01:24:30.343 --> 01:24:31.687
Question 17.

1493
01:24:31.967 --> 01:24:35.679
How do you set maxReplicas for every service across a platform?

1494
01:24:35.959 --> 01:24:37.324
Thirty-second answer:

1495
01:24:37.604 --> 01:24:53.753
Per service, take the minimum of: the connection budget of each shared store divided by pool size (summed across services sharing it), half the USL-fitted peak from the latest load test, consumer partition counts for consumers, and third-party rate limits.

1496
01:24:54.033 --> 01:25:00.753
Record it in the Helm values, check it in CI (including sums across services), and verify it at startup.

1497
01:25:02.103 --> 01:25:03.277
Question 18.

1498
01:25:03.557 --> 01:25:08.122
Cell-based architecture: what is it and when would the grocery platform need it?

1499
01:25:08.402 --> 01:25:09.767
Thirty-second answer:

1500
01:25:10.047 --> 01:25:20.266
Split the whole stack (services + data) into independent cells, each serving a subset of users (for example a group of cities), with a thin routing layer in front.

1501
01:25:20.546 --> 01:25:24.898
A bad deploy, a noisy tenant or a data corruption affects one cell.

1502
01:25:25.178 --> 01:25:33.157
Consider it when blast radius, regional scale or compliance demand it, typically at millions of users across many regions.

1503
01:25:34.507 --> 01:25:35.744
Question 19.

1504
01:25:36.024 --> 01:25:39.437
How do organizational structure and scalability interact?

1505
01:25:39.717 --> 01:25:41.083
Thirty-second answer:

1506
01:25:41.363 --> 01:25:44.392
Conway's law: systems mirror team communication.

1507
01:25:44.672 --> 01:25:51.072
A single deployable shared by 8 teams limits delivery throughput, which is an organizational scaling limit.

1508
01:25:51.352 --> 01:25:56.493
The Y-axis split exists as much for team independence as for runtime scaling.

1509
01:25:56.773 --> 01:26:01.893
Conversely, splitting services without team boundaries creates distributed monoliths.

1510
01:26:03.243 --> 01:26:04.353
Question 20.

1511
01:26:04.633 --> 01:26:08.601
Present a 2-year scalability roadmap for the platform to leadership.

1512
01:26:08.881 --> 01:26:10.246
Thirty-second answer:

1513
01:26:10.526 --> 01:26:14.003
Start from the forecast (10 times growth) and measured limits:

1514
01:26:14.283 --> 01:26:24.737
USL fits per tier-1 service, database runway (months until the primary's limit versus migration lead time, Ch 02), and third-party limits.

1515
01:26:25.017 --> 01:26:41.998
Sequence the work by runway minus lead time: remove σ or κ hot spots now, vertical-scale databases, add caches and read models, partition carts and inventory by city next, orders by region later, cells if regional blast radius demands it.

1516
01:26:42.278 --> 01:26:43.921
Give cost and risk per step.

1517
01:26:45.271 --> 01:26:46.167
Part 9.

1518
01:26:46.447 --> 01:26:47.855
Summary and Practice.

1519
01:26:49.105 --> 01:26:50.193
Key takeaways.

1520
01:26:50.473 --> 01:26:52.350
Scalability is designed in.

1521
01:26:52.630 --> 01:26:55.766
A system scales only as far as its shared parts allow.

1522
01:26:56.046 --> 01:27:00.889
Vertical first for stateful stores, horizontal from day one for stateless tiers.

1523
01:27:01.169 --> 01:27:04.262
Amdahl: serial work caps speed-up at 1 per second.

1524
01:27:04.542 --> 01:27:07.209
USL: coherency makes throughput peak and fall.

1525
01:27:07.489 --> 01:27:11.009
Fit σ and κ from load tests and set limits from N.

1526
01:27:11.289 --> 01:27:17.283
Stateless services: no local sessions, files, unmanaged caches or unguarded jobs.

1527
01:27:17.563 --> 01:27:20.294
Test statelessness by killing pods under load.

1528
01:27:21.644 --> 01:27:29.431
Identity in tokens, sessions in Redis if needed, domain state (carts) as first-class data with atomic operations.

1529
01:27:29.711 --> 01:27:34.063
Scheduled jobs: lease lock with an owner token, plus idempotent work.

1530
01:27:34.343 --> 01:27:44.049
Connection budgets are the hidden ceiling. maxReplicas = min(budget ÷ pool, N/2), enforced in CI and at startup.

1531
01:27:44.329 --> 01:27:45.353
Scale cube:

1532
01:27:45.633 --> 01:27:49.281
X clones, Y splits by function, Z partitions data.

1533
01:27:49.561 --> 01:27:51.204
Real platforms combine them.

1534
01:27:51.484 --> 01:27:58.439
Partition moves need fencing: version the map, fence the old location, move lazily, sweep, then finalize.

1535
01:27:59.789 --> 01:28:04.013
Climb the 1, then 1 M journey on measured triggers, and don't skip ahead.

1536
01:28:05.363 --> 01:28:06.685
Decision cheat sheet.

1537
01:28:06.965 --> 01:28:08.309
Here is a quick table:

1538
01:28:08.589 --> 01:28:10.040
Situation, and Choose.

1539
01:28:10.320 --> 01:28:12.517
Stateless tier needs more capacity:

1540
01:28:12.797 --> 01:28:17.363
X-axis clones + concurrency-based autoscaling, capped by budgets.

1541
01:28:17.643 --> 01:28:19.499
Throughput stops rising with pods:

1542
01:28:19.779 --> 01:28:24.451
USL fit, then remove σ (locks, hot keys) / κ (broadcasts).

1543
01:28:24.731 --> 01:28:26.416
Stateful store at its limit:

1544
01:28:26.696 --> 01:28:30.195
Scale up first; then partition (Z) with a move protocol.

1545
01:28:30.475 --> 01:28:31.712
Session-like state:

1546
01:28:31.992 --> 01:28:36.536
Tokens for identity; Redis for sessions; first-class storage for carts.

1547
01:28:36.816 --> 01:28:38.715
Periodic job on many pods:

1548
01:28:38.995 --> 01:28:42.771
Lease lock + idempotency (or a Kubernetes CronJob).

1549
01:28:43.051 --> 01:28:44.651
Big city dominates load:

1550
01:28:44.931 --> 01:28:47.896
Dedicated partition; split by zone when needed.

1551
01:28:48.176 --> 01:28:49.520
Team-level bottleneck:

1552
01:28:49.800 --> 01:28:52.232
Y-axis split along bounded contexts.

1553
01:28:52.512 --> 01:28:54.155
Regional blast radius:

1554
01:28:54.435 --> 01:28:56.056
Cells (Ch 34).

